A temporary sentinel node makes the first position look like every other position. The sentinel sits before the head, so insertion always uses the same four link updates. The real head is returned at the end.

This also keeps the edge cases simple: `K = 1` inserts before the old head, while an invalid position leaves the list alone.

```go
/** Definition for a Node.
type ListNode struct {
    Data int
    Next *ListNode
    Prev *ListNode
}
*/

func InsertBeforeKthPosition(head *ListNode, X int, K int) *ListNode {
    if K < 1 {
        return head
    }

    if head == nil {
        if K == 1 {
            return &ListNode{Data: X}
        }
        return head
    }

    sentinel := &ListNode{Next: head}
    head.Prev = sentinel

    target := sentinel
    for position := 1; position <= K; position++ {
        if target.Next == nil {
            return head
        }
        target = target.Next
    }

    before := target.Prev
    inserted := &ListNode{
        Data: X,
        Prev: before,
        Next: target,
    }

    before.Next = inserted
    target.Prev = inserted

    result := sentinel.Next
    result.Prev = nil
    return result
}
```

Time complexity: `O(K)` to reach the target.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space for the sentinel and a few pointers.

This version first counts the nodes. After that, it chooses a direction for finding the kth node. If the position is closer to the tail, walking through `prev` links saves some pointer moves.

The new node is then linked between `target->prev` and `target`. The head changes only when the insertion position is 1.

For example, in `4 <-> 8 <-> 12 <-> 16`, inserting `10` at `K = 3` finds `12` and produces `4 <-> 8 <-> 10 <-> 12 <-> 16`.

```cpp
/**
class ListNode
{
 * Definition for doubly-linked list.
 *  public:
 *      int data;
 *      ListNode *prev;
 *      ListNode *next;
 *      ListNode() : data(0), prev(nullptr), next(nullptr) {}
 *      ListNode(int x) : data(x), prev(nullptr), next(nullptr) {}
 *      ListNode(int x, ListNode *prev, ListNode *next) : data(x), prev(prev), next(next) {}
};
*/

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* insertBeforeKthPosition(ListNode* head, int X, int K) {
        if (K < 1) {
            return head;
        }

        if (head == nullptr) {
            return K == 1 ? new ListNode(X) : head;
        }

        int length = 0;
        ListNode* tail = head;
        while (tail != nullptr) {
            ++length;
            if (tail->next == nullptr) {
                break;
            }
            tail = tail->next;
        }

        if (K > length) {
            return head;
        }

        ListNode* target;
        if (K <= length - K + 1) {
            target = head;
            for (int position = 1; position < K; ++position) {
                target = target->next;
            }
        } else {
            target = tail;
            for (int position = length; position > K; --position) {
                target = target->prev;
            }
        }

        ListNode* inserted = new ListNode(X);
        inserted->prev = target->prev;
        inserted->next = target;

        if (target->prev != nullptr) {
            target->prev->next = inserted;
        } else {
            head = inserted;
        }
        target->prev = inserted;

        return head;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` for the length count, with at most another `O(n)` walk.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

This one stores the existing nodes in an array first. Inserting at index `K - 1` is easy, and a final loop reconnects every `prev` and `next` pointer.

It uses more memory than direct pointer editing, but the pointer setup is straightforward and makes the first and last positions behave the same. The input nodes are reused, and only the new node is allocated.

```javascript
/**
 * // Definition for a Node.
 * function ListNode(data, prev, next) {
 *    this.data = data;
 *    this.prev = prev || null;
 *    this.next = next || null;
 * };
 */

class Solution {
  /**
   *
   * @param {ListNode} head
   * @param {Number} X
   * @param {Number} K
   * @returns {ListNode}
   */
  insertBeforeKthPosition(head, X, K) {
    if (K < 1) {
      return head;
    }

    const nodes = [];
    let current = head;
    while (current !== null) {
      nodes.push(current);
      current = current.next;
    }

    if (K > nodes.length) {
      return head;
    }

    nodes.splice(K - 1, 0, new ListNode(X));

    for (let i = 0; i < nodes.length; i++) {
      nodes[i].prev = i === 0 ? null : nodes[i - 1];
      nodes[i].next = i + 1 === nodes.length ? null : nodes[i + 1];
    }

    return nodes[0];
  }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` to collect and reconnect the nodes.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the array of node references.

A recursive helper moves to the kth node by decreasing `K` at each call. When `K` becomes 1, that node is the insertion target, so the helper updates its previous link and the link from the previous node.

The original head stays the same except when `K` is 1. The method also leaves the list unchanged if the requested position is outside the list.

```java
/*
// Definition for a Node.
class ListNode {
    public int data;
    public ListNode prev;
    public ListNode next;
    public ListNode();
    public ListNode(int data);
    public ListNode(int data, ListNode prev, ListNode next);
};
*/

class Solution {
    public ListNode insertBeforeKthPosition(ListNode head, int X, int K) {
        if (K < 1) {
            return head;
        }

        if (head == null) {
            return K == 1 ? new ListNode(X) : head;
        }

        if (K == 1) {
            ListNode inserted = new ListNode(X);
            inserted.next = head;
            head.prev = inserted;
            return inserted;
        }

        insertBefore(head, X, K);
        return head;
    }

    private boolean insertBefore(ListNode node, int X, int K) {
        if (node == null) {
            return false;
        }

        if (K == 1) {
            ListNode inserted = new ListNode(X);
            inserted.prev = node.prev;
            inserted.next = node;

            if (node.prev != null) {
                node.prev.next = inserted;
            }
            node.prev = inserted;
            return true;
        }

        return insertBefore(node.next, X, K - 1);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(K)` recursion steps.
Space complexity: `O(K)` for the recursive call stack.

